.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-SNAPSHOTS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-snapshots
.Nd which ZFS snapshots a kldload machine takes by itself, when, how many it keeps, and what they are for
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A kldload machine snapshots its running boot environment before every
package transaction and every hour, and its
.Pa /srv
every fifteen minutes, and prunes each kind to a fixed number.
Those snapshots are what
.Xr kldload-rollback 8
turns into a boot environment when an update goes wrong.
.Pp
Each snapshot's name says who took it:
.Ar dataset Ns @ Ns Ar kind Ns - Ns Ar YYYYMMDD Ns - Ns Ar HHMMSS .
.Bl -column "apt-pre, apt-post" "running boot environment" "keep" -offset indent
.It Sy kind Ta Sy of Ta Sy keep Ta Sy taken by
.It apt-pre, apt-post Ta running boot environment Ta 10 Ta
apt, before and after each transaction
.It dnf-pre Ta running boot environment Ta 10 Ta
.Ql dnf
and
.Ql yum ,
before each transaction that changes the system
.It kpkg Ta running boot environment Ta 10 Ta
.Xr kpkg 8 ,
where the wrapper below is absent
.It auto Ta running boot environment Ta 48 Ta
.Xr kldload-snapshot 8 ,
hourly
.It srv Ta Ar pool Ns /srv Ta 4 Ta
every 15 minutes
.It manual Ta any Ta 10 Ta
.Ql snapshot-create.sh manual Op Ar dataset
.El
.Pp
The newest are kept; each taker prunes its own kind after taking one.
A snapshot that a boot environment was cloned from \(em every one a
rollback used \(em cannot be destroyed while the clone exists, and is kept
and said so, not counted as removed.
.Pp
Snapshots kldload does not take \(em
.Xr sanoid 8 Ns 's
.Ql autosnap_ ,
the install snapshot, anything made by hand \(em are neither pruned nor
judged by any of this.
.Ss How the transactions are caught
On Debian and Ubuntu, apt's own hooks,
.Pa /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/00-kldload-snapshot-pre
and
.Pa -post ,
run
.Ql snapshot-create.sh apt-pre
and
.Ql apt-post ;
they fire for anything that runs apt or dpkg.
On every family,
.Pa /usr/local/bin/apt
and
.Pa dnf
are
.Pa kldload-pkg-wrapper ,
which takes the pre-transaction snapshot for the verbs that change the
system and then runs the real tool with the arguments untouched; and adds
.Ql apt rollback
and
.Ql dnf rollback ,
which are
.Xr kldload-rollback 8 .
A pre-transaction snapshot that fails does not stop the transaction:
apt's hook and the wrapper both say, loudly, that it goes ahead without a
rollback point.
Two apt hooks asking for the same name in one second is not a failure; the
snapshot is reused.
.Ss The tools
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nm snapshot-create.sh Ar kind Op Ar dataset
Take one snapshot of
.Ar kind
.Po
.Ql apt-pre ,
.Ql apt-post ,
.Ql dnf-pre ,
.Ql dnf-post ,
.Ql srv
or
.Ql manual ,
the default
.Pc ,
then prune that kind.
.Ar dataset
is for
.Ql manual
only.
A dataset that does not exist is skipped with exit 0; an unknown kind is
exit 1.
.It Nm snapshot-prune.sh Ar dataset Ar prefix Ar keep
Destroy the oldest snapshots of
.Ar dataset
whose names start
.Ar prefix ,
leaving
.Ar keep .
It exits 0 when a snapshot cannot be destroyed, and says which and why:
it runs inside the package manager's hook, where a failure would stop the
install.
.It Nm snapshot-policy.sh Op Fl v
For the running boot environment and
.Ar pool Ns /srv ,
one line per kind: count, limit, oldest, newest, and whether it is over;
and one line counting the snapshots kldload does not manage.
.Fl v
lists the managed ones.
Exit 1 when a kind is over its limit.
.El
.Pp
All three log to
.Pa /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log
and need root.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev KLDLOAD_ROOT_SNAP_KEEP
How many hourly
.Ql auto
snapshots
.Xr kldload-snapshot 8
keeps; default 48.
.It Ev KLDLOAD_LOG_DIR
Log somewhere other than
.Pa /var/log/kldload ;
for tests.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log
What was taken and pruned, and why a prune kept something.
.It Pa /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/00-kldload-snapshot-pre , Pa 00-kldload-snapshot-post
apt's hooks.
.It Pa kldload-snapshot.timer , Pa kldload-srv-snapshot.timer
The hourly and fifteen-minute takers.
.El
